{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Mirror inventory finding F11 (`reports/stability/mirror-inventory.md`), LOW.\n\n## Problem\n\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py` `ROUTES` — the live route names\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_execute.py:22` `_CROSS_SESSION_WARN_COMMANDS` — 14 hardcoded SPELLINGS, used at `:74`\n\nAll 14 are live route names today, so this is preventive. A route RENAME silently drops the\nwarning: `:74` is `if name not in _CROSS_SESSION_WARN_COMMANDS`, so a stale spelling simply never\nmatches and the cross-session warning disappears with nothing failing.\n\n## Scope — what is and is not the mirror (epic AC2)\n\nWHICH verbs warn cross-session is a DISTINCT CONCEPT: a policy subset, the same shape that makes\n`_NOT_CLAIMABLE_STATUSES` not a mirror. This ticket does not touch that judgement and must not\nchange which commands warn.\n\nThe mirror is ONLY the SPELLINGS, which must be route names and are not enforced to be. This is\nthe one CLI policy subset not folded into `derive_policy_sets` (`_registry.py:680`), where every\nother such subset already lives.\n\n## Approach — add-route-flag\n\nGive `Route` a `warn_cross_session` flag, set it on those 14 routes, and fold the set into\n`derive_policy_sets` beside its siblings. The spellings then ARE route names by construction, and a\nrename carries the flag automatically.\n\n## The census test this change must update\n\n`derive_policy_sets` has a key census that is pinned exactly:\n`tests/unit/test_cli_registry.py:81` asserts `set(derived) == set(_CENSUS_SETS)` against the\nhardcoded 17-name `_CENSUS_SETS` tuple at `:28`. Adding a derived key makes that assertion fail\nuntil the tuple names it, so this change MUST update that test — it is declared in `file_impact`\nfor exactly that reason, and leaving it out is what would make the close gate refuse the ticket\nfor going beyond the reviewed plan.\n\nThe new key is an OVERLAY set (its members already appear in a base set, like `_CONFIRM_SCOPE`\nand `_LEGACY_OUTPUT`), so it also belongs in the skip list inside `_all_current_spellings`\n(`:74`), which would otherwise double-count its members in the spelling census.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: the warn set is derived from `ROUTES` via `derive_policy_sets`; `_execute.py` no longer\n      carries a spelling list. Verified by a test asserting the derived set is used and the literal\n      is gone.\n- [ ] AC2: the membership is UNCHANGED — exactly the same 14 commands warn. Verified by a test\n      pinning the derived set against the 14 names the literal carried, so the refactor cannot\n      quietly change policy.\n- [ ] AC3: every warn-flagged name is a live route name. Verified by a test asserting the derived\n      set is a subset of live `ROUTES` names, which is the property a hardcoded list could violate.\n- [ ] AC4: renaming a route carries its warning. Verified by a test that renames a flagged route in\n      a synthetic registry and asserts the derived set follows the new name.\n- [ ] AC5: the `derive_policy_sets` key census still passes. Verified by\n      `test_derive_covers_exactly_the_census_set_names` being green with the new key named in\n      `_CENSUS_SETS`, and by the spelling census not double-counting the overlay members.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit -k \"registry or execute or cross_session or policy\" -q` — AC1-AC4.\n- `make lint && make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788412273886066001,"uuid":"778e76b8-0573-4da8-8214-6135edbe21e3"}